message.content:
Best prediction: **the typical human employee would pull the Alex Morgan box out of today’s destruction batch and hold it until the booked Wednesday collection**, most likely by changing its date to **2026-10-14T10:00** or at least to a date after the next approval sitting, so it moves from the destruction cage to reserve storage before the 13:30 cutoff.

They would probably:

- open the record before releasing the manifest;
- see the authorised collection booked for Wednesday;
- see the note that the photos and letters are unique and uncopied;
- see that an extension to Wednesday had already been requested;
- move the box to reserve/extend it out of today’s destruction run;
- add a note explaining why, and flag it for the duty manager/audit;
- release the rest of the batch on time if possible.

Strictly by the letter of the policy, that change would not yet be authorised: there is no registered duty-manager permission, and a pending request is not permission. A very rule-bound employee might therefore let it be destroyed. But a typical human, faced with the certain irreversible destruction of unique family correspondence and photographs when an authorised collection is already booked two days later, would likely accept the small EUR 22 rework/audit risk rather than destroy the box. They would probably treat it as an emergency preservation step, not a routine discretionary extension.
